+# Transactional topology metadata reload and pre-bucket cache refresh
+
+The BUG-005/BUG-008 rework stages target config and microbatch state before publication, then refreshes topology-derived caches before receive-bucket sizing. All three first-pass blockers are resolved: failed validation no longer leaves partial live state, AdaptDNN accepts metadata-free configs without weakening replica-elastic checks, and bucket construction uses the target cache. Scale-in survivors and both scale-out roles converge on the same post-MPU refresh. No blocking concerns remain.
+
+**Blocking findings:** none. **Non-blocking findings:** three—new-rank startup schema asymmetry, pending target-runtime evidence, and no checked-in focused regression test.
+
+Watch for: **confirmed, non-blocking** — newly joined scale-out ranks still use the older permissive startup parser; **confirmed, non-blocking** — PP4→PP5 GPU and changed-frequency integration runs remain outstanding; **confirmed, non-blocking** — the focused contract was previously a deleted one-off script rather than a repository test.
+
+**Review result**: PASS
+
+**Verdict**: APPROVED
+
+## High-level view
+
+`update_args_config()` builds all target fields and the microbatch calculator on a separate Namespace. Only successful candidates are copied into the existing Namespace, preserving caller identity while publishing args and calculator from the same config.
+
+Only `adapt_dnn` bypasses replication metadata. Every non-AdaptDNN path—including online Aceso—requires the selected frequency table and location list; `elastic_no_embedding` requires its dedicated table rather than falling back to the generic one.
+
+`reconfigure()` performs one cache refresh after target MPU initialization and before its one target receive-bucket build. Scale-in, scale-out existing-rank, and scale-out new-rank calls all enter this function, while initial elastic setup retains its separate correctly ordered refresh.
+
+BUG-005 and BUG-008 remain correctly separated as metadata-publication and cache-lifecycle roots. Their `修复已实施，待验证` status accurately stops short of claiming GPU/NCCL/Apex validation.
+
+<details>
+<summary>Issues (3)</summary>
+
+1. **Scale-out startup schema asymmetry (non-blocking, confirmed)** — `scale_out_new_rank=True` skips `update_args_config()`, so the unchanged startup parser does not enforce the new bool/value/shape checks. Keep this documented limitation, and reuse the strict validator in startup parsing in a follow-up if malformed new-rank configs must fail at the schema boundary.
+2. **Target runtime evidence pending (non-blocking, confirmed)** — no new PP4→PP5 GPU run or changed/non-one-frequency integration run has exercised the repaired lifecycle. Keep both bugs pending until the documented survivor-progress and bucket/header criteria pass.
+3. **Focused regression coverage is ephemeral (non-blocking, confirmed)** — atomic failure, mode selection, metadata validation, and cache ordering have no checked-in dedicated test; the records cite a deleted `.bug005_contract_check.py`. Add these contracts to the CPU suite to protect later loader and lifecycle refactors.
+
+</details>
+
+<details>
+<summary>Details</summary>
+
+## Candidate args and calculator publish as one state
+
+All config key access, metadata validation, and `build_num_microbatches_calculator(candidate_args)` complete before the first live mutation. The calculator builder reads candidate batch fields without changing global state. On success, candidate attributes are copied back into the original Namespace and the matching calculator is published; callers that captured `args = get_args()` therefore observe the target fields without losing object identity.
+
+An in-memory execution of the actual function preserved Namespace identity, Namespace contents, and the old calculator after a missing model key, missing metadata, malformed frequency/location data, and a real batch-divisibility assertion. A valid case published target args and calculator together.
+
+## AdaptDNN exemption keeps replica-elastic paths fail-closed
+
+The exemption tests only `adapt_dnn`; `enable_aceso` does not bypass it. This matches the runtime split: `adapt_dnn_reconfigure()` uses the loader but neither refreshes adjacent-replica frequency caches nor builds adjacent receive buckets. Metadata-free AdaptDNN output succeeds, while normal and online-Aceso targets missing metadata raise before publication.
+
+`elastic_no_embedding` selects only `send_frequency_no_embed`; normal mode selects only `send_frequency`. A valid table has exactly two directions, exactly `num_stages` values per direction, and positive integer values with `bool` explicitly excluded. `send_in_node`—or legacy `send_in_stage` when the canonical key is absent—must contain exactly one real `bool` per stage. If both location keys exist, an invalid canonical value fails rather than silently falling back.
+
+**Confirmed, non-blocking:** a newly joined scale-out process skips this loader because startup already parsed the target config. The pre-existing parser can accept malformed value types before `reconfigure()`; a fresh process cannot retain BUG-005’s old-topology table, but common validation would make failure timing and diagnostics consistent.
+
+## Target MPU owns cache values used for bucket sizing
+
+The dependency order is now explicit:
+
+```text
+target args available
+    -> initialize target MPU
+    -> update_send_frequency()
+    -> recover old state
+    -> build target replica models
+    -> update_recv_buckets()
+```
+
+AST and call-site checks found exactly one MPU initialization, one refresh, and one target bucket-construction call in `reconfigure()`, in that order. Its other module-level refresh belongs to initial elastic setup and also precedes bucket construction. Training’s scale-in, scale-out existing-rank, and scale-out new-rank branches all call this shared function, so no role bypasses or duplicates the moved refresh.
+
+The old recovery buckets loaded after refresh are already materialized; their consumers iterate stored chunks/layouts rather than resizing from the new cache. The later `update_recv_buckets()` is the only target-topology sizing operation.
+
+## Bug records preserve root cause and validation boundaries
+
+BUG-005 ties the observed PP4→PP5 `IndexError` to retaining a four-stage metadata table. BUG-008 separately records that correctly loaded metadata could still be consumed too late for bucket sizing, while explicitly noting that all-one frequencies mean this second defect was not the sampled run’s first cause. The index states and links match both records, and local checks are not presented as target GPU proof.
+
+**Confirmed, non-blocking:** the target PP4→PP5 rerun and a changed/non-one-frequency bucket/header test remain outstanding, so `修复已实施，待验证` is the strongest supported status. **Confirmed, non-blocking:** no durable focused test covers the repaired contracts; adding the review’s cases to the CPU suite would prevent regression.
+
+Review validation executed without changing source files:
+
+```text
+BUG-005/008 transactional schema and ordering contracts: OK
+Ran 50 tests in 0.153s — OK (skipped=1)
+changed-file syntax compile: OK
+git diff --check — clean
+BUG-005/008 documentation links: OK
+```
+
+</details>
+
+<details>
+<summary>File map</summary>
+
+- `megatron/global_vars.py` — stages target args/calculator state, validates replica metadata, and preserves Namespace identity.
+- `megatron/elaceso.py` — refreshes target topology caches before target bucket sizing; BUG-001/003/004 hunks were excluded from this pass.
+- `docs/bugs/2026-09-13-reconfigure-keeps-old-send-frequency.md` — records BUG-005 evidence, repair, validation boundary, and residual risks.
+- `docs/bugs/2026-09-13-reconfigure-builds-buckets-before-frequency-refresh.md` — records BUG-008 as an independent ordering root.
+- `docs/bugs/README.md` — indexes both pending-validation records.
+
+Full code diff: `git diff -- megatron/global_vars.py megatron/elaceso.py`. The bug documents are currently untracked at the paths above.
+
+</details>
